{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"sericate-chitchatty-arawana","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Context / Problem\n\nInvestigation under `dissocial-windowy-kusimanse` (`3394-43b7-4c32-49bd`) established that the tickets branch carries a **duplicate parallel lineage**: 28,570 commits whose content overwhelmingly re-expresses events already present on the other reachable side. Of 30,740 paths in the graft-side net change, 30,730 already existed at the pre-graft tip with identical path and identical blob SHA.\n\nThat investigation identified what **delivered** the duplicate lineage — GitHub Actions run `34514936676` (`Reconcile Bridge`), via `run_bridge()` -> `_delivery_command()` -> `python -m rebar._store.push commit-and-push`. The delivering commit `924a93f21edf6c4066339feeb7fff2294244d917` is a 1-parent commit, not an auto-reconcile merge.\n\n**It did not establish what created the fork in the first place.** The reconciler delivered a duplicate lineage that already existed. The origin of that lineage is unknown.\n\n## Why this matters\n\nNothing currently prevents a recurrence. Until the forking mechanism is understood, any reclamation of the existing duplicate is a one-time cleanup against a process that may simply produce another one. The observed instance cost roughly a 101,186 KiB step in the pack in under thirty minutes, which was enough to push CI's authorship gate past its limit.\n\nThe reclamation work itself is tracked on `3394`. This ticket is the prevention half, and the two are independent: reclaiming without understanding the cause leaves the defect live, and understanding the cause without reclaiming leaves the pack oversized.\n\n## Candidate mechanisms\n\nTo be tested rather than assumed. The `3394` investigation did not evaluate these:\n\n- A reset, missing, or rolled-back high-water mark or sync cursor causing re-import of already-imported history\n- A re-clone against an empty or partial local store, where the reconciler could not see existing events and recreated them\n- A changed or ambiguous identity causing events to be written under a fresh lineage\n- A force-update or non-fast-forward push that orphaned a lineage which was later re-delivered\n- Concurrent reconciler passes racing on the same delivery\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. The mechanism that created the parallel lineage is identified, with evidence — not a plausible narrative, a demonstrated cause.\n2. A determination of whether the mechanism is still live in the current code and configuration.\n3. If still live, either a fix or a documented control that prevents recurrence, with a test that would fail if the control regressed.\n4. A determination of whether any lineage fork other than this one exists in the store's history. One instance was found by investigating a pack-size anomaly; a smaller fork would not have been noticed by that route.\n5. A detection mechanism for future forks that does not depend on someone investigating a pack-size step after the fact.\n\n## Constraints\n\nThe tickets branch is rebar's live production store, shared by concurrent sessions. Investigation is read-only with respect to it. Policy forbids ad-hoc raw git mutation in the tracker; `rebar tracker-maintenance` is the supported door if mutation is ever required.\n\nPer `docs/plan-review-gate.md` and the `project.portability` criterion, any detection or prevention control must be operation-linked or in-process. A control whose only trigger is a specific CI system is not portable and will not pass review.\n\n## Notes\n\nDivergence between the two lineages is small but runs in both directions: 2 comment files exist only on the graft side, 37 files only on the pre-graft side, and 8 `.bridge_state/*` paths differ. Any theory of the fork's origin has to account for divergence in both directions, which rules out a simple one-way replay.","id":"82fb-fd2c-c221-40a5","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Origin of the duplicate tickets lineage is unknown, so recurrence is unprevented"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1789686190153445060,"uuid":"46cdb431-5ca7-47d6-a507-400027ae987f"}